<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Our customers' billing accounts live in somebody else's system, behind HTTP. The tempting thing is a `BillingClient` module with `get_account/1` called from wherever it is needed. Do not. That client has no types, no policies, no code interface, no notifications, no place for an agent to discover it, and every caller handles its errors differently.

Instead, **model it as a resource with no database**. Callers use actions, exactly like any other resource. The HTTP is an implementation detail behind those actions. When the vendor changes, or the data moves in-house, callers do not change.

We will use [JSONPlaceholder](https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users) as the "billing system".

## A manual read

`Ash.DataLayer.Simple` means "no storage of my own". A `manual` read implements fetching; Ash still handles arguments, policies, calculations, and loading.

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Billing.Client do
  # The only module that knows the wire. Swap it in tests with Req.Test.
  def req, do: Req.new(base_url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com", retry: :transient)
end

defmodule Tutorial.Billing.Account.Read do
  use Ash.Resource.ManualRead

  def read(query, _data_layer_query, _opts, _context) do
    path =
      case query.arguments do
        %{id: id} when not is_nil(id) -> "/users/#{id}"
        _ -> "/users"
      end

    case Req.get(Tutorial.Billing.Client.req(), url: path) do
      {:ok, %{status: 200, body: body}} -> {:ok, body |> List.wrap() |> Enum.map(&to_record/1)}
      {:ok, %{status: 404}} -> {:ok, []}
      {:ok, %{status: s}} -> {:error, "billing API returned #{s}"}
      {:error, e} -> {:error, e}
    end
  end

  defp to_record(m),
    do: struct(Tutorial.Billing.Account, id: m["id"], name: m["name"], email: m["email"], company: m["company"]["name"])
end

defmodule Tutorial.Billing.Account do
  use Ash.Resource, domain: Tutorial.Billing, data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Simple

  attributes do
    integer_primary_key :id
    attribute :name, :string, public?: true
    attribute :email, :ci_string, public?: true
    attribute :company, :string, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    read :read do
      primary? true
      argument :id, :integer
      manual Tutorial.Billing.Account.Read
    end
  end

  calculations do
    # An Elixir calculation: there is no SQL behind this resource, so no fragments.
    calculate :email_domain, :string, fn records, _ctx ->
      Enum.map(records, &(&1.email |> to_string() |> String.split("@") |> List.last()))
    end
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.Billing do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Tutorial.Billing.Account do
      define :list_accounts, action: :read
      define :get_account, action: :read, args: [:id], get?: true
    end
  end
end
```

Note what we did not write: no JSON-to-type casting for `email` beyond the struct (Ash types it), no pagination glue for callers, no error shape per caller.

Fetch account `3` through the code interface.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  Tutorial.Billing.get_account!(3)
  ```

  </div>
</details>

## Filters and sorts still work

With `Ash.DataLayer.Simple`, Ash applies filters and sorts in memory after your manual read returns. So callers can write ordinary queries:

```elixir
require Ash.Query

Tutorial.Billing.Account
|> Ash.Query.filter(contains(company, "LLC"))
|> Ash.Query.sort(name: :desc)
|> Ash.read!()
|> Enum.map(& &1.name)
```

For large remote sets, push the filter down instead: read `query.filter` in the manual read and translate the parts the API supports. That is an optimisation; correctness does not depend on it.

## Remote writes are generic actions

Most APIs expose verbs, not rows. A **generic action** is a typed verb with arguments and a return type, and it gets policies and a code interface like any other action.

Add a generic action `:issue_credit` to `Tutorial.Billing.Account` taking `account_id :integer` and `amount :decimal`, which POSTs to `/posts` (JSONPlaceholder echoes it back) and returns `:map`. Define `issue_credit` on the domain.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  action :issue_credit, :map do
    argument :account_id, :integer, allow_nil?: false
    argument :amount, :decimal, allow_nil?: false, constraints: [greater_than: 0]

    run fn input, _ctx ->
      body = %{userId: input.arguments.account_id, amount: Decimal.to_string(input.arguments.amount)}

      case Req.post(Tutorial.Billing.Client.req(), url: "/posts", json: body) do
        {:ok, %{status: 201, body: b}} -> {:ok, b}
        {:ok, %{status: s}} -> {:error, "billing API returned #{s}"}
        {:error, e} -> {:error, e}
      end
    end
  end
  ```

  Domain: `define :issue_credit, args: [:account_id, :amount]`. Then `Tutorial.Billing.issue_credit!(3, Decimal.new("25.00"))`.
  The `greater_than: 0` constraint refuses a negative credit before any HTTP happens.

  </div>
</details>

## Relate local to remote

A `Ticket` (local, ETS or Postgres) can `belongs_to :billing_account, Tutorial.Billing.Account`, attribute type `:integer`. Ash loads across data layers, so `Ash.load!(ticket, :billing_account)` calls your manual read. The rest of the tutorial treats the billing system as just another resource.

## Why this matters later

* **Agents (14, 22):** `ash_agent_tools` describes `issue_credit` like any action, and an agent can validate inputs before calling it.
* **Processes (21):** a BPMN service task invokes actions through the host's `ActionInvoker`. "Issue the credit" is an action, so the process never sees HTTP.
* **Idempotency:** process nodes may run twice under Oban redelivery. Real credit APIs need an idempotency key. Add an `:idempotency_key` argument and send it as a header; that is the action's contract, not the caller's problem.
* **Integration hubs:** in `ash_enterprise`, third-party APIs arrive through an integration hub (ADR 0011). The hub is plumbing; the resource is still the interface.

